"--- name: joycraft-session-end description: Close an authorized Joycraft implementation session, report evidence honestly, and archive a wholly accepted feature only at the session boundary. compatibility: Requires Node.js 22+ and the installed .joycraft runtime for the archive helper. --- # Joycraft Session End Start with the clean-tree check: run `git status --porcelain`. When it prints anything, the tree holds the user's work: report what the steps below find, fix nothing, commit nothing of the user's and push nothing. Run each step in its read-only form: `status.mjs` without `--fix`, `cleanup.mjs tidy` to report, the prune preview without `--apply`, the archive preview without `--apply`. Gate messages, cleanup fixes and the lease release also wait. When every spec of the queue is done, run `node .joycraft/runtime/close.mjs --feature <slug> --session <id> --context <pages>` and act on what it prints. Use the steps below only when it refuses or the queue is not finished. Read each touched queue and report the real state of every affected spec: `todo`, `in-review`, or `done`. `done` must have either an accepted PASS report receipt or a completion-helper owner-acceptance record bound to a current failed or unverified report; a commit, PASS command, or implementer assertion is not acceptance. Keep blocked or failed dependency branches explicit while identifying independent ready work that can continue next. If the session stops or restarts a concurrent run, report its coordinator ownership, refs, worker trees, leases, locks and processes. Release only what that run owns; keep ambiguous merges, locks, processes and trees for diagnosis. Do not force local state to a remote tip. Start a replacement only from the run journal and a new dispatch plan. For each completed spec, report the implementation revision, independent reviewer session ID, report and receipt paths, actual commands and outcomes, visual inspection status where required, and known limitations. For owner-accepted work, also report the owner-acceptance record and its inherited limits as `done, owner-accepted with <limits>`; preserve the original `FAIL` or `UNVERIFIED` report rather than calling it PASS or verified. List each spec completed under a feature waiver as `done, waived by the user: \"<instruction>\"`, with any report's real verdict; never call it reviewed or verified. If a completion operation failed or was interrupted, do not declare success or hand-edit statuses. Keep the helper's error and journal, then follow the recovery in [completion guidance](../../../.joycraft/references/completion.md). Leave the paths and next actions another session needs to resume the ready queue without redoing settled context or decisions. ## Records Run `node .joycraft/runtime/status.mjs --fix` (repairs lagging frontmatter of accepted specs, lists features ready to archive) and `node .joycraft/runtime/gate.mjs check`. Do the step each gate message names, such as a completing command. Tell the user each message you could not clear. Ask the user each status `questions` entry (findings rule: `$joycraft-verify`). Write each answer where it lasts: a decision in the decision log; a requested change in a new spec or the held spec. Never archive a feature whose question the user asked to wait. When an `UNVERIFIED` review identifies only an environment condition, record that condition and arrange a fresh review after it is corrected. Do not create an unchanged-code fix round. Route each reusable fact the session established (a decision, a trap, a reference, a production fact) to its one home, as [knowledge guidance](../../../.joycraft/references/knowledge.md) says. Leave feature intent in the brief. Write nothing temporal into an entry file or a guide: a date, a status or a hand-off goes to its record, as the [cleanup reference](../../../.joycraft/references/cleanup.md#what-to-find-on-each-page) says. When independent reports suggest a reusable failure lesson, follow [recurring failure guidance](../../../.joycraft/references/recurring-failures.md). Run `joycraft-cleanup` in its session-end mode. Put its other findings on the session-end page; with none, say so in one line. Commit the records and cleanup's fixes in their own commit, `docs: session-end records`, before tidy: `tidy --verify` refuses every changed file that no fixable finding names, so a record cannot ride in the tidy commit. With no record or fix written, skip it. ## One tidy commit Tidy without being asked. Run `node .joycraft/runtime/cleanup.mjs tidy` and fix only the lines its `fixable: true` findings name, never an entry file or rule text. When `cleanup.mjs tidy --verify` passes, make one commit with the message it prints; it carries only the tidy fixes. With no fix, it prints no message: make no commit. ## Worktree prune Preview `node .joycraft/runtime/worktrees.mjs prune` and apply it with `--apply`: it removes only clean, integrated trees of done specs and keeps the rest with their reason. Report kept trees and their size from `list`. When this session coordinated a queue and no work on it remains, release the lease with `node .joycraft/runtime/coordinator.mjs release --queue <queue> --session <your-session-id>`. See [worker isolation](../../../.joycraft/references/worktrees.md) when the session used parallel mutable workers. ## Archive last Archive only after the host has ended active work for a feature. It is never a completion action: do not invoke it from `complete`, `verify`, a hook, or while a worker or unfinished dependent needs the feature paths. Read [archive guidance](../../../.joycraft/references/archive.md) before preparing the host attestation. Use the archive CLI's default preview first. It requires `phase=session-end`, a quiescent host attestation, durable constraints already carried into current context, every queue spec `done`, accepted independent PASS reports or historical owner-acceptance records bound to their retained failed/unverified reports, intact historical receipts, and no completion journal. Apply is the separate explicit step: ```sh node .joycraft/runtime/archive.mjs archive --feature <slug> --phase session-end --attestation <attestation.json> node .joycraft/runtime/archive.mjs archive --feature <slug> --phase session-end --attestation <attestation.json> --apply ``` Keep historical reports as they are. Never describe old code hashes as a new acceptance. If queue or spec edits after acceptance block archival, find the cause. Use the archive-reconciliation path in the archive guidance when the owner authorized it. Record the instruction, affected report and input hashes, cause, resolution and limits; keep every original report and accepted version. Do not ask again for authorization you have. Do not rewrite acceptance hashes, roll back a queue to satisfy old reports, or re-review done work only to move a folder. Report `archived with reconciliation`, never renewed acceptance. Unresolved product work and corrupt or missing evidence still block closure. After `--apply`, fix the tests the preview listed in `namedFiles` (see the archive guidance), then commit the move and those edits in their own commit, `docs: archive <slug>`. That commit takes every archive edit: the move, the live docs it relinked and the path edits in the tests the preview listed. ## One push Then make one push that carries these commits and opens the PR, or updates the open one. With no remote, make the same commits, skip the push and say the work is local. After merge, session end does nothing more: say that the next session start checks, read-only, that the change deployed and works. "

